<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olaf III of Norway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1050–<a href="/years/1093">1093</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Olaf Kyrre, Olaf the Peaceful</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Olaf III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, known as Olaf Kyrre ('the Peaceful'), was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a> and survived his father's defeat at Stamford Bridge in 1066. King of Norway from 1067 to 1093, he kept the kingdom at peace, encouraged trade and the growth of towns, and is traditionally credited with founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>. His reign also strengthened the church, with fixed bishoprics in Nidaros, Bergen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olaf-iii-of-norway.jpg" alt="Olaf III of Norway" width="320" height="142" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stenersen, L. B (1843-1921) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olav_Kyrre_mynt_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Olaf was a son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-hardrada">Harald Hardrada</a> and Tora Torbergsdatter. He joined his father's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1066 but was only 16 at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stamford-bridge">Battle of Stamford Bridge</a> on 25 September 1066 and stayed to guard the ships at Riccall, so he did not fight. After the defeat he sailed with the remains of the Norwegian force to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, where they wintered, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in the summer of 1067. He then shared the kingdom with his brother Magnus II, who had become king the previous year. When Magnus died in 1069, Olaf became sole ruler.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> enjoyed a rare extended period of peace under Olaf. He renounced an offensive foreign policy and protected the kingdom's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> through agreements and marriage connections. When the Danish king Svend Estridsen prepared to attack after the defeat of 1066, Olaf made peace with him and married his daughter Ingerid, and he also made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson</a>'s Heimskringla, he founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>, and he took measures so that key cities could better serve as royal residences.</p><p>Unlike his father, who quarrelled with the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a>, Olaf fully recognised the archbishop's authority over the Norwegian church. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a>, who had travelled with the king's court, established fixed residences in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>, Nidaros and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a>, and the king took the initiative for churches including Christ Church in Bergen and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidaros-cathedral">Nidaros Cathedral</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trondheim">Trondheim</a>. He is credited with instituting the system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>, and the law of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulating">Gulating</a> was probably first written down in his time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Olaf died of illness on 22 September 1093 at Haukbø in Rånrike, then part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nidaros-cathedral">Nidaros Cathedral</a>. His marriage to Ingerid was childless, and he was succeeded by Magnus III, called Barefoot, acknowledged as his illegitimate son. Medieval sources describe him as tall, fair and silent in public but cheerful at drinking parties and peaceful throughout his reign. A Norwegian silver coin found in Maine in 1957, the Maine penny, has been dated to his time, though how it reached a Native American village there is unclear and disputed. An abstract equestrian statue of him by Knut Steen was unveiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bergen">Bergen</a> on 21 May 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Olaf III of Norway” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. Died in Ranrike, in what is now <a href="/countries/sweden">Sweden</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=59.93,10.68&amp;year=1077" aria-label="Norway on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4774 155 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Olaf III of Norway"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5224 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5224 425h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=59.93,10.68&amp;year=1077">What was here in 1077? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Now I know my own capacity, and how little I am suited to be the leader; so I would rather you should go, with my help and assistance.&#8221;</p><footer>Words to King Canute the Holy of Denmark (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Olaf Kyrre) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri Sturluson's account; declining to lead an invasion of England, where his father had fallen &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;In the days of my father these people were subjected to much terror and fear; the most of them concealed their gold and their precious things, but now I see glittering on his person what each one owns, and your freedom is my gladness.&#8221;</p><footer>Words at a guild feast (Snorri Sturluson, Heimskringla, Saga of Olaf Kyrre) &#183; tr. Samuel Laing, 1844 &#183; Snorri Sturluson's account; the son of Harald Hardrada, he was called Olaf the Peaceful &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/598/pg598-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
